#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# count_distinct

#% text
# `count_distinct(xs)` gives the number of distinct elements of `xs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Elements are compared by identity: `1` and `1.0` are distinct. `count_distinct(xs, f)` counts the classes that `unique(xs, f)` keeps: an element is new unless `f(y, x)` is true for an element `y` kept before it.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
count_distinct([1, 2, 1, 3, 2])
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count_distinct([a, a, a])
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
count_distinct([])
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# With a test:

#% cell
count_distinct([3.0, 3.1, 3.11, 3.5], (a, b) => abs(a - b) <= 0.01)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count_distinct([1, 1.0])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
count_distinct(characters("mississippi"))
#% output
#= 4

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `unique`, `tally`, `count`, `length`
